AB  - We studied symptoms, signs and the cycle of the frosty pod rot caused by Moniliophthora roreri (Cif and Par) (Evans et al.)\nin Theobroma cacao L. fruits, in the Costa-Soconusco region, Chiapas, Mexico. Moniliophthora roreri is able to develop a complete\ncycle in an average of 183 days, reaching a high incidence of the disease due to the fungus ability to infect all stages of fruit development.\nThe fruits were more susceptible at the age of one to three months, showing deformation, stains and the formation\nof a sporulanting structures; with damage ranking from 80 to 100% in fruits with four or five months, the only symptom was\nvisible tissue discoloration. Regarding the time it takes for symptoms and sings to manifest, it was observed that the number\nof days in which they appear is greater than those reported for other regions
