AB  - In Ecuador, CCN51 cocoa is very important for the national economy due to its significant contribution to the generation of foreign exchange from exports. Its productivity is diminished by the attack of many diseases in which the monillia is the most important, since it attacks mainly the cocoa bean, causing economic losses to farmers. The research is based on the comparison of 2 fungicides to control moniliasis (Moniliophthora roreri), in cacao (Theobroma cacao L.), Canton las Naves Province of Bolivar, whose general objective is to evaluate two fungicides to control the incidence of monilla in the cultivation of cocoa CCN51, in the parish of Las Mercedes. For the distribution of treatments in the field, the experimental design Completely Randomized Design (DCA) with five replications was used. Duncan's test ( = 0.05) was applied to compare the means between treatments of the selected variables. For which, in situ tests were carried out with 2 chemical fungicides, Copper Sulfate Pentahydrate and Chlorothalonil, on the control of the fungal disease Moniliophthora roreri, in cocoa (Theobroma cacao L.). The evaluations were made in the inhibition of the diseases for each of the treatments. The records made were marked for each of the variables as detailed below. Fallen flowers in which averages ranging from 25.7 to 25.3 flowers were obtained, plant flowers with averages of 71.40 to 70.80 flowers, number of diseased fruits with averages of 3.1 to 2.7 fruits, number of healthy fruits with averages of 2.9 to 2.0 fruits and in yield averages of 0.11 to 0.09 kg were obtained. The fungicide based on Copper Sulfate Pentahydrate was the one that controlled the monilla in the best way, obtaining an average of 2.9 healthy ears harvested, while Chlorothalonil obtained 2.2 healthy ears. Keywords: Agronomic management, Productivity, Phytosanitary control, diseases
