AB  - Cacao (Theobroma cacao L.) production in Ecuador is significantly affected by moniliasis caused by Moniliophthora roreri, a disease responsible for major yield losses. Due to the limitations of chemical control, sustainable alternatives such as biological control are needed.This study evaluated the antifungal activity of secondary metabolites produced by Trichoderma asperellum against M. roreri under in vitro conditions. The pathogen was isolated from severely infected cacao pods, and metabolites were obtained through submerged fermentation using hexane, methanol, and n-butanol as extraction solvents. A completely randomized design with a 3 × 4 + 1 factorial arrangement was applied, including three solvents, four doses (200, 300, 400, and 500 μL), and one control treatment. Mycelial growth inhibition was assessed 14 days after inoculation. Results showed significant effects of solvent, dose, and their interaction (p ≤ 0.05). The highest inhibition (47.34%) was obtained with the n-butanol extract at 500 μL. Inhibition increased with higher doses, with n-butanol showing the greatest effectiveness. These findings highlight the potential of T. asperellum metabolites as a sustainable alternative for cacao moniliasis management.
