AB  - Ecuador is one of the world's leading exporters of Theobroma cacao; however, it is facing a severe crisis in the cocoa sector. Production in the coastal region has decreased by more than 80% due to the appearance of the pathogen Moniliophthora roreri This phytopathogenic fungus has caused a significant economic loss, estimated at approximately 20 million dollars annually. The present study, conducted in the province of Manabí, Ecuador, aims to characterize morphologically and molecularly the Moniliophthora roreri pathogen isolated from the surface of Theobroma cacao pods. Morphological characterization revealed that the colonies exhibit variability in color, texture, and edges during growth, indicating that these parameters are not entirely reliable for phenotypic identification. In the molecular characterization, DNA of the fungus was extracted and amplified using PCR with ITS1 and ITS4 primers, which confirmed the identity of the fungus as Moniliophthora roreri with an identity percentage greater than 99%.
