AB  - Moniliasis is a challenging disease to control in cacao crops due to the asymptomatic period of the fungus, which complicates its detection, despite the use of chemical fungicides and cultural management practices. Therefore, it is necessary to explore new control methods, such as biological methods. In the present investigation, the behavior of Trichoderma harzianum and Trichoderma asperellum against Moniliophthora roreri, the causative agent of moniliasis in cacao cultivation, was evaluated. A Completely Randomized Design with three treatments and five repetitions, totaling 30 experimental units, was used. The percentage of radial growth inhibition and antagonistic capacity in dual culture on Petri dishes were assessed. The data obtained for the experiment were evaluated with Generalized Mixed Models and a mean comparison test using Fisher's LSD. In the dual culture assay, treatments T1 (Trichoderma harzianum) and T2 (Trichoderma asperellum) showed no significant differences against M. roreri C1, with inhibition percentages of 53.95% and 54.46%, respectively. On the other hand, T1 against M. roreri C2 showed a higher inhibition percentage of 50.02% compared to T2 with 42.33%.
